event: content_block_delta
data: {"type":"content_block_delta","index":1,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"Moving carts out of the cookie and into a server-side store fixes the 4 KB limit either way. Redis gives you fast reads and writes, and expiry comes built in via TTLs, which suits abandoned carts. Its weakness is durability: unless you configure persistence, a restart or eviction can lose carts, and if you don't already run Redis, it's a new system to operate. Postgres keeps carts durable, lets you query them for analytics or recovery emails, and needs no new infrastructure if you already run it. Its costs are slightly slower writes on every cart change and a scheduled job to purge stale carts. I'd recommend Postgres, since carts are business data that shouldn't disappear, and it adds no new system. Redis is the better fit only if carts are purely ephemeral and Redis is already in your stack."}}

